A||N||6||Deer_Slayer||Fiero Question||||5||
Z||000000||Deer_Slayer||02-16-2000||03:58 PM||||Yesterday, an ad came up in my local ad bulletin for an 87 Fiero.  The ad read:<BR>  <BR>87 fiero, black, tan interior, good shape, automatic, new windshield, 4cl, needs motor, good tranny, $400.<P><BR> I thought of calling the guy, but I got some adverse thoughts just before.  I wasn't sure if I could work on it at this time because of a tight budget.  What do you all think?    TIA<BR>      ||||reg||5
Z||000001||BOILERMAKER||02-16-2000||04:14 PM||||Personally (now this is only my personal thoughts) I won't put any money down on a 4cylinder.  However, I got a call last night from a person in my neighborhood that told me his fiero was for sale.  In his message he said "well hell, if you want it for free, you can come and get it".  Now, this is a 4 cylinder, and I will take it off of his hands, but that is the only way I would take a 4 cylinder.<P>BTW, I may have a 4 cylinder engine you can have.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||02-16-2000||04:27 PM||||I got the experience of driving a 4-cyl Fiero a couple of weeks ago, and I loved it. As far as I know the engine was completely stock, but the way I drive(like an old grandma), there was no noticeable difference. I'm sure I could tell it was a 4-cyl if I "drove" it. But the owner was in the car and it is his daily driver. I would hate to hurt something that isn't mine and is depended upon by the owner.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||02-16-2000||05:29 PM||||I've got two V6 Fieros, but had the opportunity to drive an 84 2M4 for several hundred miles last year.  From the Dells to Loda , Ill.  I thought the 4 banger with unknown milage, the odometer said 89000, but it was stuck, was a blast to drive. It seemed to like the high rpms. It handled nicely.  The 4 cylinder was made as an econobox commuter car, not a dragster nor a Daytona race car.  The 4 cylinder engines, when taken care of last forever.  You can do quad 4 conversions if you want more power.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||02-17-2000||01:23 AM||||Yep, the four bangers can last forever, even when not quite taken care of..  My duck's odo stopped working at least 5 years ago at 72,000 and I'm guessing it has at least 110,000-120,000 miles on it, never rebuilt.  It's an auto so it's kind of a dog off the line(no offence dogtired  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) but once the revs get up there it moves pretty good for a four.  <BR>These 4's also had 130 ft/lbs of torque, so you should be able to jump most of the tiny 4cyl imports off the line.  but after 30 feet the 7,000rpm redline of an import will probably make quick work of the duke, assuming a skilled driver... although skilled drivers are pretty rare, especially in imports..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> imho!..||||reg||1
Z||000005||dogtired||02-17-2000||02:38 PM||||IMHO (I think)<BR>$400 is a little high for a car with a bad motor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Both my daily drivers are V6s, but last month I drove a 84 4cyl and it was fun.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Due to the price of gas I would like to find a good little 4cyl to restore?? There isn't a thing wrong with the 4s and are fairly economical to work on, unless you want to do lots of tricks then there goes $$$$$<BR>Don't know what to tell you about your tight budget, It seems I always have more time than money? And now I don't even have much time??    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||theogre||02-17-2000||08:00 PM||||My 87 4 has over 140,000 on it. puts out allot more than you'd expect. Torque multiplication in an automatic tranny makes it pretty quick off the line. No race car, but fast enough. <P>DIS 2.5 motors from other GM cars (Pontiac 6000 and Olds Cutlass for a start.) can be used in Fiero. You will likely need to change some pullys and brackets around. You'll also need to change the thermostat neck, and maybe the idler pully and bracket for the throttle cable. The rest is minor stuff. Nothing hard, especially with the motor on the floor. just time consuming. <P>you might need to take off the water pump to change the pully. If you do just replace the waterpump at the same time. ((Waterpumps that sit for a long time often don't last long.))<P>Warning: if you have to disconnect the fuel lines from the throttle body, and I think you do, then you MUST use 2 wrenches or you can crack the TBI unit. ((Even if it's a fiero motor it may be easier to get them of the TBI than fight with the disconect under the battery.))<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-17-2000).]||||reg||1
